  "apod": {
    "date": "2026-10-29",
    "title": "The Horns of Venus",
    "explanation": " Explanation:   That's no moon.  But you could be forgiven for thinking it was a  crescent Moon  in the sky. It is a lesser known fact that  Venus also has phases . (What about  other planets? ) The  featured image  shows  Venus  seen almost directly between the Sun and the Earth. This happens once every  year and a half , and it is like the alignment that happens every month during a new Moon. But the new Moon is dark, while Venus' backlit  atmosphere   refracts and scatters  the sunlight, creating a glowing  crescent arc  that extends beyond 180 degrees: the tips of this arc are called the \"horns\". The crescent can be seen with a small telescope or binoculars during twilight, but those with  exceptionally acute eyesight  may be able to see it with the unaided eye. Ancient  Babylonian  astronomers may have been the first to study Venus' brightness changes, which are related to its  phases .  Many other cultures  have similar astronomical records, including the  oldest   surviving   Maya book .    Tomorrow's picture:  What's next?",
    "image": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/venus_20261024a.jpg?w=744&h=744&fit=clip&crop=faces,focalpoint",
    "link": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-29-october-2026-the-horns-of-venus/",
    "credit": " Thierry Legault ",
    "alt": "See Explanation.  Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.",
    "media_type": "image"
  },
